# Claude Code (ignore everything except shared team skills)
# settings.json is gitignored for public-publishability — it reveals the
# founder's personal toolchain. If your project needs cross-machine
# permission sync, do it via a private path outside the repo, not via
# the public repo.
.claude/

# Per-engineer agent roster. Works like .env: AGENT_ROSTER.example.md is the
# tracked template, AGENT_ROSTER.md is your personal copy. Each engineer runs a
# different fleet (different agents, subscriptions, quotas), so the live roster
# must not be shared or overwritten by a blueprint sync.
#   cp AGENT_ROSTER.example.md AGENT_ROSTER.md
AGENT_ROSTER.md

# Test coverage
coverage/

# ============================================================
# PUBLIC-PUBLISHING PRIVACY BLOCK
# ------------------------------------------------------------
# struct2flow projects are designed to be publishable on public GitHub
# without leaking AI configurations or operational state. The rules
# below partition the worktree into PUBLIC (tracked, pushable) and
# PRIVATE (local-only). Anything that reveals the founder's identity,
# AI workflow, or operational state lives PRIVATE.
#
# Lifecycle artifacts under docs/ (BACKLOG.md, BUGS.md, SLICE-*/PLAN.md,
# FEATURES.md, ACCEPTANCE_TESTS.md, requirements/, waiting-acceptance/,
# done/, mocks/, all folder README.md) are PUBLIC by default. Only the
# four methodology files inside docs/ are PRIVATE — see below.
#
# Before pushing to a public remote, read docs/PUBLISHING.md (itself
# gitignored below) for the §1b index preflight and §3a fresh-repo
# allowlist publish flow.
# ============================================================

# --- struct2flow / multi-AI methodology (PRIVATE) ---
# These files reveal how the founder works with Codex + Claude Code.
# Lifecycle artifacts under docs/ stay PUBLIC; only the four
# methodology files inside docs/ are listed here.
/CLAUDE.md
/AGENTS.md
/AGENT_SIGNAL.md
docs/DoD.md
docs/PUBLISHING.md
docs/doing/HANDOVER.md
docs/**/CODEX_REVIEW.md
/project_config_overview.md
/project_config_paths.md
/project_config_dod.md

# BUG-009 — the patterns above are ANCHORED with a leading slash on purpose.
# They name files at the repository ROOT. Written bare, a gitignore pattern
# matches at every depth, so `templates/project_config_*.md` — the seed source —
# was silently skipped by `git add` and a PR went out with an empty templates/.
# `git add` reports nothing when it skips an ignored path, so the only signal was
# CI failing on a missing template. Keep the slashes.

# A-27: security + infra were omitted while docs/PUBLISHING.md's preflight has
# always required ALL FIVE `project_config_*.md` to be ignored. They are the two
# most sensitive of the set — the threat model, adversary assumptions and
# incident playbook, and the infra account IDs and state backends — so on a
# derived project's first public push they were exactly the wrong two to leak.
# (In the blueprint itself all five stay tracked as templates: git ignores
# .gitignore for already-tracked paths, which is how the other three already
# behave.)
/project_config_security.md
/project_config_infra.md

# struct2flow agent-protocol scripts (Codex dispatcher etc.).
# Project-specific scripts that ship publicly go in scripts/ with
# names that don't match these.
scripts/codex-signal-watch.sh
scripts/start-codex-signal-watch.sh
scripts/new-project.sh

# Bootstrap provenance — points at the founder's local blueprint path
# and the source commit SHA. Created by scripts/new-project.sh on
# bootstrap.
.blueprint-source

# Project-specific ignores: add below in your project's own .gitignore extension.
# Do NOT edit them between the blueprint markers — they'd come back on next sync.

# Agent activity feed logs (scripts/agent-activity.sh)
logs/

# Agent scratch space — throwaway probes, debug harnesses, commit-message
# drafts. Kept INSIDE the repo so the work is visible next to the code that
# prompted it, rather than hidden in a system temp dir. Never committed.
# Bootstrap ships `git archive HEAD` (A-05), so nothing here can leak into a
# derived project.
.scratch/
